# BUG-292: `old <parameter>` is refused, because it is inert.
#
# A parameter cannot change between entry and exit — Zebra rejects both ways of
# trying (`n = 999` is "cannot assign to constant", `var n = 999` is "shadows
# function parameter"). So `old n` is exactly `n`, and an `ensure` written this way
# passes under ANY implementation of `old`, including a broken one.
#
# This is the shape QUICKSTART §24 used to DEMONSTRATE the feature with, which is how
# the defect was found: the canonical example sat in the one position where it
# provably cannot do anything.
#
# Registered `smoke_tc_fail` asserting the MESSAGE, not merely that it errored — the
# message is the whole deliverable here, since the refusal exists to teach the fix.
#
# NOTE the registration is also what keeps `divergence` honest: smoke_tc_fail is the
# DERIVED source of that gate's "selfhost rejects by design" list, and that is exactly
# true of this file (the bootstrap does not have the check). Contrast BUG-294's probe,
# where the same registration would have been a lie.
#
# Positive control: test/bug292_old_field_test.zbr — `old` on a FIELD still compiles
# and RUNS, so this refusal cannot be passing because `old` broke generally.
def increment(n: int): int
    ensure
        result == old n + 1
    return n + 1

def main()
    print("v=${increment(1)}")
